 },
 "beats": [
  {
   "type": "title",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "globe",
   "text": "How did one ship stop world trade for six days?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In March 2021, the giant container ship Ever Given got stuck sideways in the Suez Canal, in Egypt."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Where exactly is the canal?",
   "scene": "today",
   "date": "Today",
   "place": "Egypt"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It cuts across Egypt, from Port Said on the Mediterranean to Suez on the Red Sea."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "About a hundred and ninety-three kilometres of water, through the desert.",
   "scene": "canal",
   "date": "",
   "place": "Suez Canal"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Why does it matter so much?",
   "scene": "routes",
   "place": "Asia to Europe"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Without it, ships between Asia and Europe sail all the way around Africa. That's thousands of kilometres more."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Who dug it?",
   "scene": "lesseps",
   "date": "1859",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.4,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.33,
     1.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Pharaohs and Persian kings dug early canals from the Nile to the Red Sea. The modern one was the dream of a French diplomat, Ferdinand de Lesseps."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Work began in 1859. Huge numbers of Egyptian peasants were forced to dig, many by hand. How many died is still debated.",
   "scene": "works",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It opened in November 1869, with a grand ceremony.",
   "scene": "opening",
   "date": "17 November 1869",
   "place": "Port Said",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.35,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.6,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So Egypt got rich?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Not exactly. Egypt fell deep into debt, and in 1875 it sold its shares in the canal to Britain."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "When did Egypt take it back?",
   "scene": "crisis",
   "date": "1956",
   "place": "Port Said",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In July 1956, President Nasser nationalised it. Britain, France and Israel invaded. Under pressure from the United States and the Soviet Union, they pulled out."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "The canal stayed Egyptian.",
   "scene": "end",
   "date": "1956",
   "place": "Egypt"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "After the 1967 war it closed for eight years. Fifteen ships were trapped in the Great Bitter Lake the whole time.",
   "scene": "bitterlake",
   "date": "1967 – 1975",
   "place": "Great Bitter Lake",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.45,
     1.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And the Ever Given?",
   "scene": "evergiven",
   "date": "23 March 2021",
   "place": "Suez Canal",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.45,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It blocked the canal for six days. Estimates say billions of dollars of trade were held up every day."
